WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.400
What do these two movies have in common? More&nbsp;
than you might think production-wise at least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.040
The scene where the creature swims below the&nbsp;
unsuspecting Kay is not only beautiful and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.080
iconic- it helped craft another famous water-based&nbsp;
horror scene. Steven Spielberg borrowed elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.080
from the scene for JAWS' opening moments. Some&nbsp;
scenes are even shot for shot. Each woman dives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.800
into the water and there's a long shot of them&nbsp;
swimming happily until the perspective shifts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.880
focus on the female swimmer from the monster's&nbsp;
point of view. Sure we get to see Gill-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.040
in The Creature from the Black Lagoon, and not&nbsp;
Spielberg's shark. But both films have a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:40.000
mashup of haunting beauty and abject terror.
All the better to emphasize the fear of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.160
lurks below the water. Oh and another fun fact:&nbsp;
the cry the shark lets out at the end after it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.102
blown up? Yeah that's a modified version&nbsp;
of Gill-Man's roar!

00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:50.857
 "Smile you son of a-"

